.TH PVE\-EXT\-PATCH 8 "2026-09-08" "pve-ext" "Proxmox VE"
.SH NAME
pve\-ext\-patch \- apply, verify, remove and report pve\-ext managed patches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pve\-ext\-patch
.RI [ \-\-root
.IR dir ]
.RI { apply | remove | verify | status }
.RI [ manifest ...]
.br
.B pve\-ext\-patch
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pve\-ext\-patch
is
.BR pve\-ext 's
generic, TOML\-manifest\-driven managed\-patch tool (pve\-ext is Proxmox
VE's generic extension layer for adding API modules, UI pages and file
patches without a one\-off dpkg\-divert/\fBperl\ \-c\fR setup per project):
it dpkg\-diverts a stock
Proxmox VE file away, regenerates the "real" path as pristine original plus
a unified diff in a scratch copy, gates installation on a syntax/template
check, and only then installs the result over the real path. It replaces
writing bespoke dpkg\-divert / \fBpatch\fR(1) / \fBperl\ \-c\fR machinery once
per project with one declarative TOML manifest per set of patched files.
.PP
Manifests are TOML files, normally installed under
.IR /usr/share/pve\-ext/patches/ *.toml
by whichever package owns them (pve\-ext ships its own two\-file manifest;
other packages, e.g.\& pve\-meta, ship their own). Each manifest may declare
a top\-level
.B id
field (its stable claim identity, see "CLAIM IDENTITY" below) and any
number of
.B [[file]]
tables, each with a
.BR path ,
optional
.BR package " (informational only),"
.BR diff " (a unified diff, relative to the manifest's own directory),"
.BR marker " (a literal string used by status/verify and, for a template"
check, as the exact tag expected exactly once in the output), and an
optional
.B check
.RI ( perl ,
the default, or
.IR template ).
See
.I pve\-ext/README.md
in the pve\-meta source tree for the full manifest format and worked
examples.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI apply\ [manifest...]
Diverts each entry's file (if not already diverted), applies its diff with
.B "patch \-p1 \-\-fuzz=0"
into a scratch copy, gates on a clean \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR and then the
entry's
.B check
(perl\ \-c, or a template marker/\fB</body>\fR check), and only then
.BR install (1)s
the result over the real path. Best\-effort per file: one file's anchors
moving upstream never blocks the others in the same or a different
manifest. Refuses \(em with a clear error, never a silent merge \(em to
patch a file a \fIdifferent\fR manifest has already diverted (see
"LIMITATIONS" below). Must run as root, unless
.B \-\-root
is given.
.TP
.BI remove\ [manifest...]
Restores each entry's pristine file and removes its diversion. Must run as
root, unless
.B \-\-root
is given.
.TP
.BI verify\ [manifest...]
Dry\-runs
.RI ( "patch \-\-dry\-run" )
each entry's diff against its pristine copy (the diverted backup if
applied, otherwise the live file, assumed pristine) and reports
hunks\-would\-apply versus hunks\-failed. Makes no changes to the target
tree; safe to run unprivileged and against
.BR \-\-root .
.TP
.BI status\ [manifest...]
Reports, per file: whether it is diverted and by whom, whether the
pristine backup is present, which manifest currently claims it, whether
the marker is present in the live file, and whether the live file is
byte\-for\-byte in sync with pristine\ +\ diff.
.PP
With no
.I manifest
arguments, every command acts on every
.I *.toml
file found under the manifest directory (see "FILES" below).
A
.I manifest
argument is either a bare name (resolved to
.IR name .toml
under that directory) or a path to a manifest file directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root\ dir
Resolve every path this tool touches \(em including the files it patches,
its dpkg\-divert diversions, its per\-path claim markers, its scratch
backups on failure, and its failure log \(em under
.I dir
instead of
.IR / ,
and pass
.B "\-\-root dir"
through to
.BR dpkg\-divert (1).
Intended for running
.B verify
(and
.BR status )
against an extracted package tree in CI without touching the live system;
.B apply
and
.B remove
also honor it for the same reason, and are safe to exercise this way
against a scratch tree. Nothing this tool does under
.B \-\-root
writes to the real filesystem, the real dpkg diversion database, or the
real syslog.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH CLAIM IDENTITY
A dpkg diversion is owned by the
.B pve\-ext
package name regardless of which manifest created it, so
.BR dpkg\-divert (1)
alone cannot tell two manifests apart. A small marker file next to each
diversion
.RI ( path .pve\-ext\-orig.claimed\-by ,
never touched by dpkg) records which manifest currently claims that path.
A manifest's claim identity is its own top\-level
.B id
field, not the filename or path it was invoked with \(em so the same
manifest content reached under two different names (a checkout path
versus its installed name) claims the same files instead of refusing
itself. A manifest with no
.B id
field falls back to its filename basename, with a warning on every
invocation.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No two manifests may patch the same file: this is a known, intentional
limitation, not a bug. Composing two independent diffs on one file safely
is real work this tool does not attempt; if two packages need to change
the same file, fold the change into the manifest that already owns it, or
patch a different file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success. Non\-zero if any selected file failed to apply/remove/verify,
if no manifest or no
.B [[file]]
entries were found, or for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/pve\-ext/patches/*.toml
Installed manifests (searched first when
.B \-\-root
resolves it); in a checkout, falls back to
.I ../patches
next to this script.
.TP
.I <path>.pve\-ext\-orig
The pristine backup dpkg\-divert creates for a diverted
.IR path .
.TP
.I <path>.pve\-ext\-orig.claimed\-by
The claim marker recording which manifest currently owns
.IR path .
.TP
.I <root>/run/pve\-ext\-patch/failed
Append\-only failure log (never only stderr, which a postinst commonly
swallows); \fB<root>\fR is
.I /
unless
.B \-\-root
was given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dpkg\-divert (1),
.BR patch (1);
.I pve\-ext/README.md
and
.I docs/DESIGN.md
in the pve\-meta source tree.
